/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (Technological advancements, economic and cyber-security threats have dramatically changed the battlefield in the past decades. A trend is resurfacing, predicted to grow exponentially: the private military sector.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (Already in 2007 the European Parliament's Subcommittee on Security and Defence requested a study on 'The Increasing Role of Private Military and Security Companies', a testimony to the growing concern about the subject. The market for said services is destined to grow \(Kinsey, 2006\). The trend is predicted to increase exponentially, with estimations forecasting the doubling of the sector in terms of revenue, from approximately 250 billion to 465 billion USD by 2034 \(Custom Market Insights, 2025\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 140 0 R
/T (The aim of this article is to compare the fast growing market of the private security sector to the 14th century 'Free Companies' that roamed the Northern Italian regions, highlighting a resurfacing trend that may have deep implications for the international landscape. The attempt is to delve into the socio-economic reasons that might have made )
/E (The aim of this article is to compare the fast growing market of the private security sector to the 14th century 'Free Companies' that roamed the Northern Italian regions, highlighting a resurfacing trend that may have deep implications for the international landscape. The attempt is to delve into the socio-economic reasons that might have made )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (The resurgence of the Private Military & Security sectors can be observed in a broader historical framework. This article attempts to link the current related market with a historical comparison, the Italian Condottieri - bands of organised mercenary groups roaming through the northern Italian regions around the 14th century. By analysing common socio-economic factors, the article argues that both stem from complex environments, where concentrated wealth, fragmented political authority, and insufficient public security provision are present. Moreover, parallelism can be made in structure, business model and strategic deployment of the two historically compared groups. This analysis ends with a broader observation on the impact of a growing private military sector, and how it can be connected to a deeper systemic shift in the global landscape, testimony of a gradual erosion of state actors in the international field. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (this trade flourish. In order to do so, a brief but necessary explanation of the private military company \(PMC\) and its historical counterpart will be provided. Furthermore, the article will observe commonalities between the two and attempt to indicate the socio-economic phenomena that triggered a growth in the demand of the subsequent fortune made in the field.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (Wealth and Weapons. PMCs and PSCs \(Private Security Companies\) are essentially businesses that provide a number of services, some of which would fall under the jurisdiction of national armed forces. They offer logistical support, military involvement, counselling, and security services. Their contracts can vary from training local military, to secure sensitive information and corporate assets, to even participation in proxy and state war intervention \(Kinsey, 2006\). In essence, where professional expertise over strategy, weaponry and tactical warfare is required, the private military sector thrives.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
