347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (Similarly, this trend is observable in current times with the ongoing growth of globalisation, which could arguably be traced back to technological advances and the end of the Cold War \(Kinsey, 2006\). Indeed, the European Parliament requested study directly links the surge of PMCs to the ongoing phenomena of privatisation \(Bailes et al, 2007\). Privatisation, rise of corporate interest, and surge of trade economy are all factors that contributed to the relevance of the private security sphere: the demand for security has surpassed what the public sphere is capable, and willing, to provide.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (Increasing Demand of Regional Stability and Security. The correlation between these two periods is rather interesting: the Italian municipalities had amassed incredible wealth, but lacked any sort of political stability and regional authority, and this can be identified as a major cause of the flourishing of the private security sector of the time. Similar insufficiency of regional stability in the Renaissance period matches the phenomena that surfaced after the collapse of the USSR. First, with the dissolution of the communist block, an entire sphere of )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
