/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (The erosion of the state primacy as the sole authority in the international order can be seen within the broader framework of globalism and multicultural exchange of people \(Cerny, 1998\). Indeed, immigration, globalised economy corporate interest, as well as many transnational issues \(such as water scarcity\), have greatly reduced the national states abilities to coordinate autonomously the international landscape. Mcfate \(2014\) insists that governments have to now contend with multilayered non-state actors, such as the UN, NGOs, as well as powerful private companies and transnational criminal organisations. Furthermore, the responses to the increasingly complex intricacies of modern warfare are better answered by a dynamic private sector rather than the stiff national defense structures, leading states to appeal towards companies that can stay innovative with fast pacing technological advancements. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 142 0 R
/T (Private Security Sector in the International Community. The international landscape has proven to be a breeding ground for the private security sector, and attempting to understand it implies treating it like a profitable business venture. Offering a strong answer to regional insecurity, the corporate soldiers are destined to grow in number and wealth in the near future. The public opinion on the matter is rather condemning, as well as concerned with the ethics, as already seen in the well-documented involvement of DynCorp \(American\) in prostitution rings in Bosnia in the 90s \(Bailes et al, 2007\). Scholars on the matter argue that 'There is plenty of evidence that private armies are more disciplined and  )
