468 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (of the current conflict in April 2023. Lebanon saw 20 aid workers killed, compared with none in 2023; Ethiopia and Syria each had 14 killings, about double their numbers in 2023. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
469 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (According to AWSD, Gaza is the most lethal context for humanitarian workers, while South Sudan, Nigeria, Lebanon, Ukraine, Ethiopia, Somalia, Syria, and the Democratic Republic of the Congo complete the ranking of the ten most dangerous countries for aid workers. Airstrikes are the leading cause of death, particularly in Gaza, Lebanon, and Ukraine, while small arms have been the most common means of violence in South Sudan and Sudan. Kidnappings have increased in South Sudan, Burkina Faso, Cameroon, the Central African Republic, Chad, Nigeria, and Sudan.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
470 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (Despite these challenges and dangers, humanitarian organizations continue to deliver vital assistance, reaching nearly 144 million people in need in just one year. But how can humanitarian assistance be secured in increasingly hostile environments? EU and UN action plans to tackle the crisis affecting the humanitarian aid sector: on May 7, 2024, the EU Council met in the 4021st session to discuss protection in humanitarian contexts. It recognized that climate change, evolving geopolitical realities, conflicts and the shrinking of humanitarian space can  increase protection risks and impact the safety, rights, and resilience of people in humanitarian )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
471 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (contexts. The Council claimed that investments are needed to develop and strengthen the protection capacity of humanitarian actors and therefore called on EU Member States to provide political support to humanitarian coordinators, national humanitarian teams, and other humanitarian leaders.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
472 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (On the other hand, the UN promotes adherence to humanitarian principles and respect for international humanitarian and human rights law. It focuses on advancing anticipatory and cost-efficient early action and rapid responses to crises. It also convenes governments, partners and communities to reduce disaster risk and losses. Despite being underfunded and under attack, the UN and its humanitarian partners worked tirelessly to deliver life-saving assistance and protection: of the $50 billion needed for humanitarian assistance, $25 billion are provided by donors. Moreover, the UN Security Council adopts resolutions to protect humanitarian personnel and ensure safe access, like the one I mentioned earlier \(Resolution 2730\), that introduced exceptional humanitarian measures within UN financial sanctions regimes.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (Protecting humanitarian workers is not only a legal obligation, but a moral imperative.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (If the world fails to protect those who save lives, it risks normalizing a future where even humanity becomes a target.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
